Log rotation logic tested through an injected filesystem seam. Save /workspace/rotate.mjs:

export function rotateLogs(files, maxBytes, io) {
  // io: { size(path), rename(from, to), remove(path), exists(path) }
  const actions = [];
  for (const path of files) {
    if (!io.exists(path)) continue;
    if (io.size(path) < maxBytes) continue;
    for (let i = 3; i >= 1; i--) {
      const from = i === 1 ? path : `${path}.${i - 1}`;
      const to = `${path}.${i}`;
      if (i === 3 && io.exists(to)) {
        io.remove(to);
        actions.push(["remove", to]);
      }
      if (io.exists(from)) {
        io.rename(from, to);
        actions.push(["rename", from, to]);
      }
    }
  }
  return actions;
}

Build rotate.test.mjs (node:test) around a makeFakeIo(initialFiles) in-memory fake: a Map from path to size, implementing all four operations and recording every call. Tests: file under maxBytes untouched; file at exactly maxBytes rotates (boundary: < means >= rotates; pin it); full cascade with existing .1/.2/.3 (assert exact ordered action list: remove .3, .2 to .3, .1 to .2, base to .1); missing base file skipped; two files processed independently; rotation with only .2 present but no .1 (holes in the sequence). Then one integration test wiring a real-fs io object (node:fs statSync/renameSync/rmSync/existsSync) against files in a mkdtemp dir, asserting the resulting directory listing. node --test green.

